tRPC routers are defined in {{#if (hasPackage "api")}}`packages/api/src/router/` and merged in `packages/api/src/root.ts`{{else}}`src/trpc/routers/` and merged in `src/trpc/routers/_app.ts`{{/if}}. Add a procedure to a router, then call it from the client via the typed `trpc` hooks. Input validation uses the router's schema, not manual checks.
Outside development, an error a procedure does not throw as a `TRPCError` (a failed query, a bug) reaches the client as `Internal server error`, without its message or stack: throw a `TRPCError` with a code to send a message. The API route, and the server-side calls that skip it ({{#if (eq stackName "tanstack-start")}}the `unstable_localLink` of `src/trpc/client.ts`{{else}}`src/trpc/server.tsx`{{/if}}), log every such error to the server output (procedure path, message, stack) in every environment, and the errors they send to the client (`UNAUTHORIZED`, `BAD_REQUEST`, ...) only in development. The API route refuses writes (any method but `GET` and `HEAD`) whose `Origin` header names another origin.
{{#if (hasLibrary "better-auth")}}

**Better Auth errors.** A Better Auth `APIError` thrown inside a procedure (`auth.api.banUser(...)` refusing with `YOU_CANNOT_BAN_YOURSELF`) reaches the client as a `TRPCError` with the code of its HTTP status and its message, mapped once by `{{#if (isMono)}}packages/api/src/auth-errors.ts{{else}}src/trpc/auth-errors.ts{{/if}}` behind `publicProcedure`; do not catch it in a router to rethrow it.

{{#if (has "database" "d1")}}
{{#if (eq stackName "tanstack-start")}}
**Session in the context.** `createTRPCContext` builds `createAuth(db)` per request, without a cookie plugin, to read the session. An instance without a cookie plugin never refreshes, so tRPC reads do not extend the session; the app's own instance extends it, through `/api/auth/get-session` (the auth client's `useSession`/`getSession`) and server code using `auth` from `src/lib/server.ts`. Only read sessions through the context's instance: an endpoint that sets cookies (sign-in, sign-up, sign-out, password change with `revokeOtherSessions`, ...) called through it would lose its `Set-Cookie` headers, so call those from the app.
{{else}}
**Session in the context.** The API route builds `createTRPCContext({ headers, db })`, which reads the session with `createAuth(db)` per request, without a cookie plugin: it never refreshes, so those tRPC reads do not extend the session. The server-side calls (`src/trpc/server.tsx`) hand the context the session the page guards already read, `getRequestSession()` of `src/lib/server.ts` (the app's own instance from `getAuth()`, with `nextCookies()`, which writes the refreshed cookie where it can and skips the refresh in a Server Component), so a page reads the session once. The app's instance also extends it through `/api/auth/get-session` (the auth client's `useSession`/`getSession`) and server code calling `getAuth()`. Only read sessions through the context's instance: an endpoint that sets cookies (sign-in, sign-up, sign-out, password change with `revokeOtherSessions`, ...) called through it would lose its `Set-Cookie` headers, so call those from the app.
{{/if}}
{{else}}
{{#if (isMono)}}
**Session in the context.** `createTRPCContext({ headers })` reads the session through the tRPC package's own instance, `packages/api/src/auth.ts`, because the package cannot import an app instance. As the tRPC library generates it, that instance is `createAuth()` with no cookie plugin: it never refreshes, so tRPC reads do not extend the session; the app's own `auth` extends it, through `/api/auth/get-session` (the auth client's `useSession`/`getSession`) and app server code. Only read sessions through it: an endpoint that sets cookies (sign-in, sign-up, sign-out, password change with `revokeOtherSessions`, ...) called through it would lose its `Set-Cookie` headers. {{#if (has "stack" "tanstack-start")}}Keep it cookie-less while a TanStack Start app uses the package: a procedure that a Start page prefetches without awaiting runs while the page streams, after the response headers are sent, so a refresh there would extend the session in the database but not in the browser.{{else}}When every app using the package runs one framework and procedures must set cookies, bind that framework's plugin in this file (`createAuth(nextCookies())`): cookie-setting endpoints then work and reads extend the session. The file shows which applies.{{/if}}
{{else if (eq stackName "tanstack-start")}}
**Session in the context.** `createTRPCContext({ headers })` reads the session through `sessionReader` (`src/trpc/session-reader.ts`), `createAuth()` with no cookie plugin: it never refreshes, so tRPC reads do not extend the session; the app's own `auth` (`src/lib/auth/auth.ts`, with `tanstackStartCookies()`) extends it, through `/api/auth/get-session` (the auth client's `useSession`/`getSession`) and the `getSession` server function. Keep it cookie-less: a procedure prefetched without awaiting runs while the page streams, after the response headers are sent, so a refresh there would extend the session in the database but not in the browser. Only read sessions through it: an endpoint that sets cookies (sign-in, sign-up, sign-out, password change with `revokeOtherSessions`, ...) called through it would lose its `Set-Cookie` headers.
{{else}}
**Session in the context.** `createTRPCContext` reads the session through the app's instance (`src/lib/auth/auth.ts`, with `nextCookies()`, which `src/trpc/session-reader.ts` exports as `sessionReader`), so a tRPC read extends the session like any app read: in a route handler the plugin writes the refreshed cookie, and in a Server Component, where cookies cannot be written, it skips the refresh.
